  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote the tower-stacking game in `src/main.ts`. It typechecks, but I couldn't run `vite build` or open it in a browser here, so it hasn't been run.\n\n- **Slabs and debris:** one box node per level plus one debris node per level, so each level keeps its own colour. The colours shift gradually by level.\n- **Moving slab:** it slides along x and gets slightly faster every 4 levels. Space or a click on the canvas drops it.\n- **Drop rules:**\n  - Only the overlap stays, and the cut-off part falls and spins away.\n  - A drop within 0.15 units of the slab below keeps the full width and gives +1 bonus (that drop scores 2).\n  - A complete miss sets `lost`.\n  - Reaching 25 stacked slabs sets `won`.\n- **Camera:** I lower the whole world as the tower grows instead of moving the camera, so the top stays in view. I did this because the docs said not to use `app.camera`, and the visible result is the same.\n- **Restart:** R, or a click on the canvas after the game ends, starts a fresh base slab.\n- **HUD and state:** a DOM bar below the canvas shows score, session best and status. `window.__TASK_STATE__` is updated on every change. `window.__TASK_READY__` is set after the first frame.\n\n**Assumption:** `height` counts only stacked slabs, so it starts at 0 and the base slab isn't counted. The task didn't say whether the base counts.\n\nI used a `click` listener rather than `pointerdown`, so synthetic click events also work.\n\n",
